You typed `google.com`. Half a second later the page appears. In between, the request travelled through your router, your ISP, fiber under the ocean, a Google data center, and back. Seven layers of protocols cooperated to make it look instant.

This chapter explains that journey. You can read it from zero — every piece of jargon is defined the first time it appears — and finish with a senior fullstack engineer's mental model: the mechanisms, the real numbers, and the failure modes that cause production outages.

Think of the internet as a stack of agreements. Each layer hides a problem from the layer above it. Wires drop electrical signals, so the link layer invents error-checked frames. Frames only travel one hop, so IP invents routing across the whole planet. IP packets get lost, so TCP invents reliable delivery. The result is an illusion: your browser hands `https://google.com` to the OS and gets back HTML, as if the world were one wire.

We follow a single request from your laptop to a Google server and back. Each section peels one layer of the illusion.

## 1. The physical layer

**The intuition.** Computers talk to each other over *something physical* — a copper wire, a glass fiber, or radio waves. That physical link is where bits actually become real-world phenomena you could measure with an oscilloscope.

**The mechanism.** Bits don't travel as bits. They travel as voltage, light pulses, or radio. Copper Ethernet carries differential voltage at hundreds of megahertz. Optical fiber turns a laser on and off billions of times per second; the light bounces along a glass strand 9 microns wide while the cable runs across an ocean floor. Wi-Fi modulates radio waves at 2.4 or 5 GHz, using <Term definition="A modulation scheme that splits one signal into many narrow sub-channels so each can be tuned independently">OFDM</Term> to pack hundreds of bits into a single radio symbol.

The numbers you reach for at this layer are the ones that bound everything above:

<Card variant="default">
  <ul class="font-mono text-sm text-bbg-annot space-y-1">
    <li>Speed of light in fiber: <strong>~200,000 km/s</strong> (slower than vacuum because glass)</li>
    <li>NYC to London ping floor: <strong>~28 ms</strong> just for the photons to make the round trip</li>
    <li>Typical home Wi-Fi: <strong>100–1000 Mbps</strong>, fluctuating with interference</li>
    <li>Data-center fiber: <strong>10–400 Gbps</strong> per pair, dozens of pairs per cable</li>
  </ul>
</Card>

<Callout tone="note" title="Speed-of-light tax">
You cannot beat physics. A New York to Singapore round trip is roughly **180 ms** just from distance. Any product that promises sub-100 ms responses to a global audience either has servers near every user (CDN) or is lying.
</Callout>

## 2. The link layer

**The intuition.** Two devices on the same wire (or radio link) need to take turns talking and notice when their words get garbled. The link layer is the local set of rules they share — like the etiquette of a phone call where you say "over" so the other side knows it's their turn.

**The mechanism.** The link layer turns a raw physical link into <Term definition="A bounded chunk of bytes with a header and a frame-check sequence">frames</Term>: small, bounded chunks of bytes with a header at the front and an error-check number at the end. On Ethernet, every frame carries a source and destination <Term definition="Media Access Control address — a 48-bit hardware identifier baked into the network card">MAC address</Term>, an EtherType label, a payload, and a 32-bit <Term definition="Cyclic Redundancy Check — a small number computed from the frame bytes so the receiver can detect tampering or noise">CRC</Term>. The CRC lets the receiver detect single-bit errors. Wi-Fi adds an acknowledgement so the sender can retransmit lost frames.

**One hop, no further.** A link-layer hop sees only its immediate neighbours. Your laptop's Wi-Fi adapter has no idea where Google is. It only knows the MAC address of the router on the other end of the radio link. When you `ping google.com`, the IP packet is wrapped inside a link-layer frame whose destination is your router, not Google.

<Stage id="stage-link" theme="lilac" title="Link layer hop" step={1} stepLabel="One frame, one hop">
  <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between gap-4 min-h-[140px]">
    <Node id="link-laptop" icon="monitor" label="Laptop" />
    <Connector from="#link-laptop" to="#link-router" label="Ethernet/Wi-Fi frame" color="lilac" drawLoop>
      <PacketDot color="#7C3AED" durationMs={1600} />
      <PacketDot color="#7C3AED" durationMs={1600} delayMs={500} />
    </Connector>
    <Node id="link-router" icon="server" label="Home router" />
  </div>
</Stage>

The router strips the link header, looks at the IP header inside, and decides where to send it next. To do that it builds a fresh link frame for the next hop — a different MAC address, a fresh CRC. The IP packet survives untouched; the link-layer wrapper changes at every hop.

<Callout tone="info" title="MTU and fragmentation">
The maximum frame size on Ethernet is **1500 bytes**. Anything larger has to be split. TCP discovers this with Path MTU Discovery and shrinks its segments. UDP that ignores the limit gets silently dropped — a classic source of "works on my machine, fails on the WAN" bugs.
</Callout>

## 3. The network layer: IP

**The intuition.** Imagine sending a postcard. You need an address everyone in the world recognises. The post office on your end forwards it to a regional sorting facility, then to a national one, then to one in the destination country, until it lands in the right mailbox. The internet works the same way, except the post offices are routers and the addresses are numbers.

**The mechanism.** Frames only know about one hop. To send data across the planet, every device needs an address that the entire internet agrees on. That's the <Term definition="Internet Protocol — addressing and best-effort packet delivery">IP address</Term> — 32 bits for IPv4 (`142.250.181.46`), 128 bits for IPv6. Routers join networks together by reading IP headers and forwarding packets toward their destination, one hop at a time.

**The chain of forwarding.** A packet's life is a sequence of forwarding decisions. Your router sends it to your ISP. Your ISP sends it through its own backbone to a regional aggregator. Eventually it crosses one of the [tier-1 networks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tier_1_network) — a handful of carriers that exchange traffic with everyone else without paying for transit. Each network is an <Term definition="A collection of IP prefixes under one administrative policy">Autonomous System (AS)</Term> identified by a number. The AS-to-AS routing table is built by <Term definition="Border Gateway Protocol — the routing protocol that glues the internet together">BGP</Term>, which advertises "I can reach 142.250.0.0/15" to neighbours and trusts them to do the same.

```
$ traceroute google.com
 1  router.lan        (192.168.1.1)    1 ms
 2  ISP gateway       (10.20.30.1)     8 ms
 3  ISP backbone      (...)            12 ms
 4  IXP exchange      (...)            14 ms
 5  AS15169 peering   (...)            16 ms
 6  Google edge       (142.250.181.46) 18 ms
```

Each line is one IP hop. Six AS boundaries, six routing decisions, all coordinated by BGP between strangers who do not even share a vendor.

<Misconception id="mc-trust">
BGP runs on trust. There is no global authority verifying that an AS actually owns the prefixes it announces. A single misconfigured router in another country can advertise "I am Google" and traffic will start flowing the wrong way until humans notice. The 2008 Pakistan-vs-YouTube incident did exactly this for two hours. Production-grade networks now use RPKI signatures, but only about half of all routes are protected.
</Misconception>

## 4. The transport layer: TCP and UDP

**The intuition.** IP just shouts packets into the network. Packets can be dropped, duplicated, reordered, or arrive corrupted. Most applications can't handle that mess directly — they want a phone call (a reliable conversation), not a stack of disorganised postcards. The transport layer is where you choose between the two.

**TCP — the phone call.** <Term definition="Transmission Control Protocol — reliable, ordered, congestion-aware byte stream">TCP</Term> turns the IP packet hose into a reliable byte stream. It opens with a three-way handshake — SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK — which costs one round trip before any application data flows. After that, every byte has a sequence number; the receiver acknowledges what it got, and the sender retransmits any gaps. A second mechanism, <Term definition="The set of rules that decide how fast the sender pushes data based on observed loss and delay">congestion control</Term>, decides how fast to push: each loss is treated as a signal that the network is full, so the sender backs off.

<Stage id="stage-tcp" theme="mint" title="TCP three-way handshake" step={2} stepLabel="One round trip to a reliable channel">
  <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between gap-4 min-h-[140px]">
    <Node id="tcp-client" icon="monitor" label="Client" />
    <Connector from="#tcp-client" to="#tcp-server" label="SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK (1 RTT)" color="success" style="solid" drawLoop>
      <PacketDot color="#16A34A" durationMs={1400} />
      <PacketDot color="#16A34A" durationMs={1400} delayMs={500} />
    </Connector>
    <Node id="tcp-server" icon="server" label="Server" />
  </div>
</Stage>

**UDP — the postcard.** <Term definition="User Datagram Protocol — connectionless, unreliable, fast">UDP</Term> skips all of this. Send a datagram, hope it arrives, move on. No handshake, no acknowledgements, no order guarantee. DNS, real-time video, gaming, and QUIC all use UDP and then rebuild whichever reliability bits they actually need. <Term definition="HTTP/3's transport — TCP-like reliability plus TLS built in, riding on UDP">QUIC</Term> looks like TCP+TLS in one but rides on UDP so browsers can ship new versions without waiting for kernels to update.

<Callout tone="warn" title="Head-of-line blocking">
TCP delivers bytes in order. If packet 47 is lost, packets 48–60 sit in the receiver's buffer until 47 is retransmitted — even if 48–60 belong to a different HTTP request multiplexed on the same connection. HTTP/3 over QUIC fixes this by making streams independent at the transport level.
</Callout>

## 5. DNS: turning names into addresses

**The intuition.** You typed `google.com`. Computers cannot deliver packets to a string — they need a number. DNS is the phonebook that maps human-friendly names to the numeric IP addresses we just talked about.

**The mechanism.** <Term definition="Domain Name System — the distributed phonebook of the internet">DNS</Term> is structured as a tree. The root servers know which servers handle `.com`. The `.com` servers know which servers handle `google.com`. Those authoritative servers return the actual A (IPv4) or AAAA (IPv6) record with the address. Your operating system rarely walks the whole tree — instead it asks a <Term definition="A DNS server that does the recursive lookup on your behalf and caches the result">recursive resolver</Term> (your ISP's, or 1.1.1.1, or 8.8.8.8) which caches answers for as long as the record's <Term definition="Time to live — how long this answer may be cached before re-asking">TTL</Term> allows.

<Stage id="stage-dns" theme="peach" title="DNS resolution" step={3} stepLabel="Cache first, recurse on miss">
  <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between gap-4 min-h-[140px]">
    <Node id="dns-os" icon="monitor" label="OS stub" />
    <Connector from="#dns-os" to="#dns-resolver" label="recurse" color="peach" drawLoop>
      <PacketDot color="#D97706" durationMs={1700} />
    </Connector>
    <Node id="dns-resolver" icon="resolver" label="Recursive resolver" />
  </div>
</Stage>

```
;; QUESTION SECTION:
;google.com.            IN  A

;; ANSWER SECTION:
google.com.       300   IN  A   142.250.181.46
```

The TTL — 300 seconds in this example — controls how long a cache may keep the answer. Short TTLs let you fail over quickly when something breaks; long TTLs save round trips but make changes propagate slowly. Most production stacks settle around 60–300 seconds for active records, with bigger numbers (hours, days) reserved for things that don't move, like the MX records of an email domain.

<Callout tone="note" title="DNS is the silent dependency">
A DNS outage looks like everything is broken. The network is fine, your servers are up, certificates are valid — but no hostname resolves. Sites that use a single managed DNS provider have been taken down by their provider's outages. Multi-provider DNS with secondary zones is the standard mitigation at scale.
</Callout>

## 6. The application layer: HTTP and TLS

**The intuition.** Now we have a path (IP), a reliable conversation channel (TCP), and we know the IP behind the hostname (DNS). What's left is the actual *language* the browser speaks to the server — and the envelope that hides that conversation from anyone in the middle. HTTP is the language; TLS is the envelope.

**The handshake.** TLS 1.3 needs one round trip. The client sends a <Term definition="The opening message of a TLS handshake; lists supported ciphers and an ephemeral key share">ClientHello</Term> with its supported ciphers and an <Term definition="Elliptic-Curve Diffie-Hellman, Ephemeral — a key exchange whose private parts are thrown away after the session">ECDHE</Term> key share. The server picks a cipher, returns its certificate, signs the key exchange, and finishes. From that round trip both sides derive a symmetric session key. Application data flows AES-GCM encrypted under that key. <Term definition="Reusing the result of a previous handshake to skip the round trip">Session resumption</Term> can compress this to zero round trips on subsequent connections.

<Stage id="stage-tls" theme="sky" title="HTTPS over the wire" step={4} stepLabel="TLS wraps HTTP, HTTP wraps the request">
  <div class="space-y-4">
    <Card variant="yellow">
      <div class="font-mono text-sm text-bbg-annot">
        <TypingText text=":method GET" />
      </div>
      <div class="font-mono text-sm text-bbg-annot mt-1">:authority google.com</div>
      <div class="font-mono text-sm text-bbg-annot">accept-encoding br, gzip</div>
      <div class="font-mono text-sm text-bbg-annot">cookie ...</div>
    </Card>
    <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between gap-4 min-h-[140px]">
      <Node id="tls-browser" icon="monitor" label="Browser" />
      <Connector from="#tls-browser" to="#tls-edge" label="HTTP/2 over TLS 1.3" color="sky" style="solid" drawLoop>
        <PacketDot color="#0284C7" durationMs={1500} />
        <PacketDot color="#0284C7" durationMs={1500} delayMs={400} />
      </Connector>
      <Node id="tls-edge" icon="lock" label="TLS layer" />
    </div>
  </div>
</Stage>

**Inside the envelope.** Once TLS is up, HTTP carries the actual request. HTTP/2 multiplexes many requests onto one TCP connection. Headers are compressed with HPACK, requests no longer block each other inside the application protocol, and the server can push responses for resources the client will ask for next. HTTP/3 keeps the same vocabulary — verbs, status codes, headers — but moves to QUIC over UDP, dropping TCP's head-of-line problem at the transport level too.

<Callout tone="note" title="The trust chain">
TLS gives you encryption *and* authenticity. The server's certificate is signed by a certificate authority your browser already trusts (the OS root store contains a few hundred). The chain of trust is what stops a Wi-Fi network at a café from silently impersonating your bank. When you see a green padlock, your browser has verified that chain in milliseconds.
</Callout>

## 7. CDN and the edge

**The intuition.** Even with a perfect protocol stack, you cannot beat physics. A round trip to a single data center on the other side of the world will always cost hundreds of milliseconds. The solution is not faster physics — it is *closer servers*. A <Term definition="Content Delivery Network — a fleet of caching servers placed close to users worldwide">CDN</Term> is a fleet of small data centers scattered around the world that pretend to be your origin server, so your users hit the nearest one.

**The mechanism.** The IP `142.250.181.46` is almost certainly not in California. It is on the edge of Google's network, a few hops from you, <Term definition="A routing trick where many servers worldwide advertise the same IP and BGP routes each user to the closest one">anycasted</Term> from dozens of physical locations to whichever <Term definition="Point of Presence — one physical location in a CDN's network">POP</Term> is nearest. Anycast lets every edge advertise the same IP prefix; BGP routes you to the closest. The first byte of your response comes from a server within a few milliseconds of you, even if the canonical origin lives in Iowa.

<Stage id="stage-cdn" theme="rose" title="Edge first, origin second" step={5} stepLabel="Anycast routes you to the nearest POP">
  <div class="relative flex items-center justify-between gap-4 min-h-[140px]">
    <Node id="cdn-user" icon="monitor" label="User" />
    <Connector from="#cdn-user" to="#cdn-edge" label="anycast 5–20 ms" color="warn" drawLoop>
      <PacketDot color="#DC2626" durationMs={1200} />
    </Connector>
    <Node id="cdn-edge" icon="cloud" label="Edge POP" />
    <Connector from="#cdn-edge" to="#cdn-origin" label="warm backbone link" color="neutral">
      <PacketDot color="#374151" durationMs={1700} />
    </Connector>
    <Node id="cdn-origin" icon="server" label="Origin" />
  </div>
</Stage>

**The trade-off.** Edge caches go stale. Invalidations need to fan out to every POP, which is a routing exercise by itself. Personalised pages have to either bypass the cache (slow) or be rendered at the edge (cheap if your runtime allows it). Modern stacks lean on the edge for static assets and pre-computed responses while letting the origin handle the long tail of dynamic data.

## 8. Putting it together

**Replaying the journey.** Now we can replay what happens when you press Enter on `https://google.com`. The browser parses the URL and notices it has no cached DNS entry, so it asks the OS resolver, which asks the recursive resolver, which walks the DNS tree until it returns an IP. With the IP in hand, the browser opens a TCP connection to port 443; the three-way handshake costs one round trip. On the same connection it negotiates TLS 1.3 in another round trip, ending with a shared symmetric key. Inside that encrypted channel it sends an HTTP/2 request — `GET /` plus a pile of headers. The packet travels through your home router, your ISP, one or two transit networks, and lands on a Google edge POP chosen by anycast. The edge has the response cached, so it streams the HTML back over the same connection. Your browser parses the HTML, fires more requests for CSS and JS, and the page paints.

**The budget.** All of that has to happen inside a budget the human eye notices. The benchmark you usually quote is <Term definition="Largest Contentful Paint — the time until the biggest visible element finishes rendering">LCP</Term>: anything under 2.5 seconds feels fine, anything past 4 feels broken. Inside that budget every layer takes a slice:

<NumbersCard
  id="card-budget"
  title="Latency budget — cold load"
  stats={[
    { label: "DNS recurse",     value: "10–150 ms" },
    { label: "TCP 3-way",       value: "1 RTT  (~25 ms)" },
    { label: "TLS 1.3",         value: "1 RTT  (0 on resume)" },
    { label: "Edge TTFB",       value: "50–200 ms" },
    { label: "First Contentful Paint",  value: "100–400 ms" },
    { label: "Largest Contentful Paint", value: "≤ 2.5 s (good)" },
  ]}
/>

Every layer below the application either consumes a chunk of that budget or absorbs the variance. The link layer takes microseconds, but a single lost frame can stall TCP for hundreds. IP routing is one-way until you measure the return path. TCP guarantees ordering but can freeze on a single missing segment. TLS adds a round trip the first time and zero on resume. Each layer has its own contract, and you only get to ignore them until something breaks.

**Where to go from here.** Each section above is a chapter in its own right. If you want to go deeper:

- *Physical and link*: read about [WDM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wavelength-division_multiplexing) and how a single fiber carries dozens of colors of light, each its own logical channel.
- *IP and BGP*: walk through a real BGP table dump — Hurricane Electric publishes one at [bgp.he.net](https://bgp.he.net). Notice how `8.8.8.8` shows different paths from different vantage points.
- *TCP*: read Vincent Bernat's [TCP performance and the Nagle algorithm](https://vincent.bernat.ch). Then look up BBR, the congestion-control algorithm Google rolled out, and why it differs from Cubic.
- *DNS*: try `dig +trace google.com` on your machine and watch each level of the tree respond.
- *TLS*: read [The Illustrated TLS 1.3 Connection](https://tls13.xargs.org). It walks byte-by-byte through a real handshake.
- *CDN*: skim Cloudflare's blog. Their post-mortems are public and they are some of the best operational writing on the internet.

<div class="flex flex-wrap items-end gap-10 my-12">
  <CountUp target={28} unit="ms" label="NYC↔London floor" />
  <CountUp target={120} unit="ms" label="TTFB typical" />
  <CountUp target={2500} unit="ms" label="LCP budget" />
</div>

<KeyTakeaway>
The internet is not a network — it is a stack of agreements. Physical links agree to move voltage. Frames agree to wrap one hop. IP agrees to forward. TCP agrees to be reliable. DNS agrees on names. TLS agrees on encryption. HTTP agrees on what a "request" means. When you watch a page load, you are watching seven layers cooperate so each layer can stay simple.
</KeyTakeaway>
